# .cursorrules - project rules for AI-Assisted Programming (Module 2)

## Project context
- Small student repo: C programs, their Python conversions, LeetCode solutions and a
  debugging exercise. Python 3.10+ and standard library only (unittest, dataclasses).

## Coding style
- Python: follow PEP 8, add type hints and a one-line docstring to every function.
- Use f-strings, never string concatenation or % formatting.
- C: C99, compile cleanly with `gcc -Wall -Wextra`, no global variables.
- Prefer clear names over comments; comment only the "why".

## Behaviour
- When converting C to Python, keep the same program output and say what changed
  (for example: no integer overflow, dataclass instead of struct).
- Explain a bug before fixing it, then fix the cause, not the symptom.
- Never edit tests to make them pass; fix the code instead.
- Show how to run any code you add (exact command).

## Testing
- Every new function gets a unittest case, including an empty or boundary input.
- Run `python -m unittest` before saying a change is done.

## Git
- Commit messages: imperative mood, subject under 72 characters, optional body
  explaining why. Example: "Fix off-by-one in highest()".
